import { BytesInspectionCopy } from "../model"

pub fn copy -> BytesInspectionCopy = BytesInspectionCopy {
  summary: "Byte represents an integer from 0 through 255. It is a different type from an ordinary Int. Use std/bytes operations rather than accessing its private representation.",
  taskTitle: "Accept only values that fit in one byte",
  purpose: "Before using a number as one byte, check that it is between 0 and 255. Accept 0 and 255, and explain why -1 and 256 fail. Here, Byte means one value; Bytes means a sequence of bytes.",
  typescriptBefore: "TypeScript uses Number.isInteger and the lower and upper bounds to validate the input. A number can also be fractional, so the integer check matters. On the successful path, value is still an ordinary number, not a separate Byte type. Passing a value straight to Uint8Array converts out-of-range values too, so rejecting them requires a check like this.",
  benefit: "Both versions are short. In Seseragi, successful validation produces a Byte, so types distinguish operations that accept Byte from those that accept an ordinary Int. Separate success from failure before using the validated value.",
  setup: "import * as bytes makes the public names from std/bytes available through the alias bytes. fn describe value: Int -> String defines a function that accepts an integer named value and returns a string. A call such as bytes.byte value puts the argument after the function name, separated by a space.",
  after: [
    "bytes.byte value returns Either<ByteError, Byte>. Here, Either is either a failure in Left or a success in Right. match chooses the branch that fits the result. The call does not return a Byte directly.",
    "Left (bytes.ByteOutOfRange invalid) examines ByteOutOfRange inside the failure and gives the rejected integer the name invalid. ByteOutOfRange is an error case, not a pattern for reading the inside of a Byte.",
    "Right checked gives the successful Byte the name checked. bytes.toInt checked is the public operation that converts that Byte to an Int. This is where the example needs its numeric value for display.",
    "A backtick string inserts a variable or a calculated result at ${...}. The string after each branch’s -> is that branch’s result, returned to the caller of describe.",
    "for value <- [0, 255, -1, 256] gives each integer in the array the name value in turn. describe value validates each one, so the first two output lines succeed and the last two report out-of-range values.",
  ],
  details: [
    "The valid range is 0 through 255, including both endpoints. Even the Int value 65 is not itself a Byte. Obtain a Byte with an operation such as byte before passing it to a function that requires Byte.",
    "There is no public Byte value constructor or Byte pattern. Besides validating with byte, you can obtain a Byte from Bytes with operations such as get. get returns Maybe<Byte>, so separate Just from Nothing in that case. Use toInt to read the numeric value.",
    "An entire Either<ByteError, Byte> is also a different type from Byte. You cannot pass the validation result straight to toInt. Receive the successful Byte first, as Right checked does here.",
  ],
}
